## 4. User Roles and Permissions

Implement real role-based authorization on the server, not merely conditional rendering in the interface.

### A. Entrepreneur

Can:
- Register and manage their account.
- Create and edit their own business profile.
- Record and edit their own sales and expenses.
- Manage their own products and inventory.
- View their own reports.
- Generate and update their own business-readiness profile.
- Access learning materials.
- Browse verified service providers.
- Submit support requests.
- Control consent for sharing their business information.

Cannot:
- View another entrepreneur's private transactions.
- Change their own role to administrator or provider.
- Access administrative functions.
- Share data with third parties without the required authorization.

### B. Business-support agent

Can:
- View entrepreneurs explicitly assigned to them.
- Help assigned entrepreneurs complete business profiles.
- Record assistance sessions.
- View only the information needed for authorized support.
- Track follow-up actions.

Must not have unrestricted access to all entrepreneurs or their financial records.

### C. Financial-service provider

Can:
- Maintain an approved organization profile.
- Publish services and eligibility information after authorization.
- Update official contact information.
- Receive referrals only when the entrepreneur has explicitly consented and the referral is authorized.

Cannot:
- Browse all user records.
- Access private transactions by default.
- View unrelated entrepreneurs' personal information.

Provider accounts must be verified or approved by an administrator before publication.

### D. Administrator

Can:
- View system-wide operational statistics.
- Manage users and approved roles.
- Approve, suspend, or reject provider profiles.
- Manage categories and educational content.
- Manage business-support agents and assignments.
- Review reports of inaccurate or inappropriate content.
- Review audit events.
- Configure public-facing information.

Administrative access must be protected by server-side authorization and a secure account-provisioning process.

Never create a public administrator registration page or hardcode an administrator password.

